<DocsLayout title="Install. Settle in. Make it yours." section="start" summary="Install LunaDash with the session installer, dismiss the welcome screen and personalize your desktop. Advanced build and validation steps are included below.">
  <h2 id="linux">Install on Linux</h2>
  <p>LunaDash is developed primarily on Arch Linux, but the installer also recognizes Debian/Ubuntu, Fedora, openSUSE, Alpine, Void and Gentoo package-manager families. Other distributions can use the same standard CMake install path after providing Qt 6.4+, wlroots 0.17–0.20, Wayland, libinput, libxkbcommon, GL, GLib, Ninja and a C++20 compiler.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={'git clone --branch dev https://github.com/LuYishan-4/LunaDash.git\ncd LunaDash\n./scripts/install-session.sh'} />
  <p>Run <code>./scripts/install-session.sh --dry-run</code> to preview the installation. <code>install-session.sh</code> delegates dependency provisioning to <code>install-dependencies.sh</code>. Arch uses makepkg/pacman; the other recognized distributions build with Ninja and install through CMake. If your package manager is unknown, preinstall the dependencies and use <code>./scripts/install-session.sh --skip-deps</code>. Quickshell 0.3+ is required for the shell and may need to be installed separately when the distribution does not package it.</p>
  <p>On non-systemd systems, install LunaDash normally but enable your display manager with the distribution's own OpenRC/runit procedure instead of <code>--enable-sddm</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="first">Your first session</h2><p>The welcome screen contains a greeting and a link to this website for help. Select <strong>Start desktop</strong> to dismiss it, or open the help link to continue in your browser. It will not appear again automatically after completion. Use Settings for language, network and appearance, or to reopen the welcome screen.</p><p>Super+Return opens the default terminal; Super+E opens the default file manager. Native X11 apps can be launched through the authenticated XWayland path. Save work before testing a standalone session.</p>

  <h2 id="test">Run the checks</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={'cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja\ncmake --build build\nenv -u MESA_GL_VERSION_OVERRIDE -u MESA_GLSL_VERSION_OVERRIDE \\\n  QT_QPA_PLATFORM=wayland ./build/lunadash-compositor --nested --socket ludash-test'} />
  <p>The repository also contains <code>./scripts/test-once.sh</code> and Wayland integration tests. Tests use isolated runtime/configuration directories. The current headless wlroots checks exercise protocol/window lifetimes and clean shutdown with pixman. The separate renderer test verifies embedded shaders and software OpenGL. A real session is still needed to inspect application content and hardware behavior.</p>

  <h2 id="coverage">Distribution coverage</h2>
  <p>Ubuntu is exercised by the main build/runtime workflows. The distribution source-build workflow configures Arch Linux, Debian, Fedora, openSUSE Tumbleweed and Alpine Edge jobs; check their completed results for the intended commit. Void and Gentoo have maintained automatic dependency/install paths but currently have less CI breadth. Generic Linux support means the standard CMake build/install path is available when the required toolchain exists; it is not a claim that every GPU, display manager or physical seat has been tested.</p>

  <h2 id="limits">Understand the preview</h2><p>Nested software-rendered sessions do not verify all GPUs or standalone login sessions. Multiple outputs, screen locking and some protocol/input integration still vary by environment. Native plugins and custom QML are disabled until explicitly trusted.</p><p>See the repository's <a href="https://github.com/LuYishan-4/LunaDash/blob/dev/docs/TESTING_AND_FILES.md">testing guide and source map</a> for sanitizer commands, platform dependencies, evidence files, packaging checks and the verification record.</p>
</DocsLayout>
